chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 19 of the 3rd edition, Keeping Time, Scheduling Tasks, and Launching Programs, teaches how a program reads the clock and waits: the time module with time.time() and time.sleep() and Project 14, the Super Stopwatch; the datetime module with datetime and timedelta objects, strftime() with the directives of Table 19-1 and strptime(); and the launching of other programs with subprocess.run() and subprocess.Popen(), command line arguments, captured output and the start and open commands, ending with Project 15, the Simple Countdown, seven practice questions and two practice programs, the Prettified Stopwatch and the Friday the 13th finder [P01] [P03]. The chapter page of 2026-10-13 is 88,909 bytes with 29 listings (20 of them interactive sessions of 75 statements), 1 captioned figure (Figure 19-1, a screenshot of six calculators, with alt text) and 1 table (Table 19-1), and its sha256, 067b9119b1a4c68cf65acedab9e68df264d609e72345fdbb112bcfd6db475db7, equals the chapterDigest of the book's own chapter ontology [P01] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The online materials"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The online materials of the 3rd edition (7,759,877 bytes, 91 files) hold three files for chapter 19, calcProd.py, stopwatch.py and simplecountdown.py, and none of alarm.wav, hello.txt, prettifiedStopwatch.py, fridayThirteenth.py or a crontab, a plist or a scheduler document [P01] [P02]. The Summary of the chapter says that the instructions for scheduling a program at a certain time are at the book's page at No Starch Press, but that page holds no such instructions (it names Scheduling Tasks only in the table of contents), and neither does the table of contents of the book online or the zip, so the one paragraph of the chapter on Task Scheduler, launchd and cron is all the book says about them [P01] [P02] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The chapter replayed"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The 75 statements of the chapter's 20 interactive sessions were replayed in order in a clean Python 3.14.6, with the zone of the process set to the author's America/Los_Angeles: 54 printed what the book prints and 21 did not, of which 7 depend on the clock (time.time(), time.ctime(), datetime.now(), fromtimestamp(time.time()) and the two values built from now), 1 is the en dash and 13 launch Windows or macOS programs or call a program that Linux lacks. With the dash repaired 55 match [P01]. The book's examples of the clock come from four different days (ctime at 11:05 on 17 March 2026, now() on 27 February 2026, fromtimestamp(time.time()) on 21 October 2026 and now on 2 December 2026), so no two of them can be compared; and in the zone of Istanbul one statement more differs, datetime.fromtimestamp(1000000), printed as 05:46:40 only in Los Angeles [P01]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Four en dashes"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Four places in three listings of the chapter as served on its web page, listings 2, 6 (twice) and 12, write the en dash U+2013 instead of the minus sign (end_time – start_time, time.time() – last_time and oct_21st – about_thirty_years), and the interpreter refuses each with SyntaxError: invalid character '–' (U+2013); a reader who copies them gets a program that does not start [P01]. The shipped stopwatch.py has two of the dashes, uses CRLF line ends and has no f prefix on its print, so a reader who fixes the dashes still prints {lap_number}: {total_time}({lap_time}) literally, unlike the chapter, whose listing has the prefix; and the stopwatch of the chapter prints the total time and the lap time without a space between them while the practice program's output has one [P01] [P02]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The profiling listing"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter times calcProd() with two time.time() readings, prints 2.844162940979004 seconds and refers to Chapter 13 of the author's other book for the method [P01] [P09]. The shipped calcProd.py is another program: it loops range(1, 100000), prints len(str(result)) and has an en dash, and its repaired form raises ValueError because Python refuses to convert an integer of more than 4300 digits to a string by default; with sys.set_int_max_str_digits(0) it prints 456569 digits, where the chapter's loop to 100001 gives 100000! of 456574 digits [P02] [P22]. On this machine the product took about 2.3 to 2.6 seconds and math.factorial(100000), which gives the same integer, took 0.136 seconds, and the chapter does not say that the digit limit stops printing the result [P24] [P10]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P09, chx:P10, chx:P22, chx:P24 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Time zone of the author"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says time.time() was called on March 17, 2026, at 11:04 PM Pacific Standard Time, but daylight saving time began in the United States on 8 March 2026, so the zone was Pacific Daylight Time at -07:00, and the printed ctime of 11:05:38 reads 23:05 on a 24-hour clock [P01] [P12] [P39]. The printed outputs of fromtimestamp(1000000), 05:46:40, are right only in Los Angeles, where the offset in January 1970 was -08:00: the same second is 13:46:40 in UTC and 15:46:40 in Istanbul, which was UTC+02:00 then; the chapter never says that the reading depends on the zone of the computer [P01] [P11]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P11, chx:P12, chx:P39 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The two-digit year"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Table 19-1 says %y is the year without century, 00 to 99, meaning 1970 to 2069, but Python and POSIX map 69 to 99 to 1969 to 1999 and 00 to 68 to 2000 to 2068, so '68' is 2068 and '69' is 1969; the book's examples, 63 as 2063 and 73 as 1973, are right either way [P01] [P10] [P34]. The chapter sends the reader to strftime.org for the directives, which now redirects (HTTP 301) to a cheatsheet of another company [P08]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P10, chx:P34 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Thirty years that are not"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's about_thirty_years is 365 * 30 days and it says it ignores leap years, but the example prints 28 October 1996 for 30 years before 21 October 2026, 7 days off because the span holds 7 leap days, and twice the span prints 5 November 1966, 15 days off the real 21 October 1966 [P01]. The text before the example calls the function datetime.delta(), which raises AttributeError; the function is datetime.timedelta() [P01] [P11]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P11 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Which clock"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter measures elapsed time by subtracting two time.time() readings, which reads the system clock: that clock can be set by an administrator or a time service, so the difference can be negative, as a simulated clock set back by 10 seconds showed (-10.0 against +10.0 on a monotonic clock), while time.monotonic() and time.perf_counter() cannot go backwards and are what the documentation and PEP 418 name for intervals [P01] [P10] [P30]. The epoch timestamp counts seconds by a formula in which a day has 86400 seconds, so a leap second has no number: 23:59:59 on 31 December 2016 and the next midnight are one second apart in epoch time, and the type refuses 23:59:60 [P10] [P34]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P30, chx:P34 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Sleep and polling"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says time.sleep() blocks until the number of seconds has elapsed; since Python 3.5 a signal handler that returns does not shorten the sleep, which is retried for the time that remains, and only a handler that raises ends it, as PEP 475 says, and the sleep can be longer than asked [P01] [P10] [P31] [P17]. The chapter's loop until Halloween 2039 sleeps one second between checks and so uses almost no processor time, which was measured (under 0.05 s in 1.2 s against over 0.1 s in 0.3 s without the sleep), but a loop that sleeps a fixed second after its work drifts: with 0.02 s of work the 3600th round started 71.98 s late [P01] [P10]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P10, chx:P17, chx:P31 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "What changed in Python"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.12 deprecated datetime.utcnow() and utcfromtimestamp(), which return naive values, in favour of now(datetime.UTC); parsing a day and a month without a year (strptime('02/29', '%m/%d') uses the year 1900) has drawn a DeprecationWarning since 3.13, and Python 3.15.0b4 raises ValueError for %d without a year; Python 3.14 added date.strptime() and time.strptime(); %:z does not exist in 3.11 [P11] [P26] [P27] [P29]. One program run under 11 interpreters (3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14.0 to 3.14.6 and 3.15.0b4) compared 48 items and 11 of them differ between releases: the two strptime methods, the encoding default, the text of an error for sleep('1'), %:z, two year-less parses, the timeout message, the category and the message of the warning for a backslash followed by A, the text of the error for timedelta(months=1) and the deprecation of utcnow [P26] [P27] [P28] [P29]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P11, chx:P26, chx:P27, chx:P28, chx:P29 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Zones and daylight saving"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter has no time zone object: the zoneinfo module of Python 3.9 and later (PEP 615) attaches an IANA zone to a datetime and reads the data of the operating system or of the tzdata package, here tzdata 2026.5 (IANA release 2026e) beside the system's 2026c, and without either ZoneInfo raises ZoneInfoNotFoundError [P12] [P33] [P39]. A timedelta of one day added to an aware datetime moves the wall clock, so noon on 7 March 2026 in New York plus a day is noon on 8 March, a span of 23 hours; the hour from 02:00 to 03:00 that day does not exist and 01:30 on 1 November 2026 happens twice, told apart by fold (PEP 495) [P11] [P32]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P11, chx:P12, chx:P32, chx:P33, chx:P39 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Launching programs on Linux"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's start and open commands are not portable: on this Ubuntu machine /usr/bin/open exists only because xdg-utils registers it as an alternative of xdg-open, start does not exist and subprocess.run(['start', 'hello.txt'], shell=True) exits 127, and with shell=True a list on POSIX runs only its first item, so ['echo', 'hi', 'there'] printed an empty line; ping -n 4 is the Windows flag and exits 2 on Linux [P01] [P13] [P45] [P46]. A command string built from a file name with shell=True ran a second command in the test ('x; echo INJECTED'), which shlex.quote prevented; webbrowser.open returned False and pyperclip raised PyperclipException with no browser and no clipboard program; playsound3 3.3.2, not the pinned 2.4.0, is the newest release [P13] [P18] [P20] [P47] [P48] [P49]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P13, chx:P18, chx:P20, chx:P45, chx:P46, chx:P47, chx:P48, chx:P49 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Scheduling, run for real"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter hands scheduling to Task Scheduler, launchd and cron in one paragraph and gives no syntax [P01]. Cron was run for real on this machine (cron 3.0pl1-184, Ubuntu 24.04): a Python job started by it two seconds after the turn of the minute saw PATH=/usr/bin:/bin, the working directory /root, 6 environment variables, no terminal, sh as its parent and a local clock of 22:36:01 UTC while the shell showed 01:36:01 +03; a TZ=Asia/Kolkata line in the crontab reached the job and set its clock to IST; a minute of 61 was refused by crontab with exit code 1 and 'bad minute' [P40] [P35]. When both day fields are restricted the command runs when either matches, so '0 0 13 * 5' fires on every 13th and on every Friday and cannot find Friday the 13th [P40]. The schedule follows the zone of the cron daemon: with the daemon started from a shell whose TZ was Europe/Istanbul an entry written on the Istanbul clock fired at the target moment and the entry for the same moment on the UTC clock did not, while the job itself saw UTC; a CRON_TZ=Asia/Kolkata line was passed to the job as a variable but did not move the schedule (the UTC entry fired and the Kolkata entry did not), and the Debian manual page does not mention CRON_TZ, which Wikipedia says an implementation may recognise [P40] [P54]. Not executed: Task Scheduler (schtasks) and launchd, whose property list was only written and read back with plistlib, and a systemd timer, whose OnCalendar expressions were only normalised by systemd-analyze because PID 1 here is not systemd [P41] [P42] [P43] [P44] [P23]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P23, chx:P35, chx:P40, chx:P41, chx:P42, chx:P43, chx:P44, chx:P54 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Appendix B"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Appendix B answers Question 3 with time.asctime(), which prints the same text as ctime() for the current time, and Question 7 with weekday() equal to 0, Monday for 7 January 2019, and both are right [P06] [P10]. It answers Question 5 with 'the closest integer', which is incomplete: round(2.5) is 2 because ties go to the even number, round(x, 2) returns a float, and round(2.675, 2) is 2.67 [P06] [P25]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P06, chx:P10, chx:P25 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The stopwatch and the countdown"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Super Stopwatch needs a terminal: run without one it raises EOFError at the first input(), and in a pseudo-terminal with scripted key presses it printed the laps of the chapter once the dashes were repaired [P01] [P02]. The shipped simplecountdown.py matches the printed one apart from spacing, but the copy served at autbor.com differs (timeLeft set to 2, camel case and the Windows start line uncommented); autbor.com/alarm.wav redirects (HTTP 302) to a 582,952-byte file of 3.30 seconds that the online materials do not hold, and no sound could be played here [P01] [P02] [P07]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P07 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Where the ideas come from"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The 1970 epoch was chosen arbitrarily: the first edition of the Unix Programmer's Manual (3 November 1971) counted sixtieths of a second from 1 January 1971 and the third edition (15 March 1972) from 1 January 1972 with a 32-bit count that 'guarantees a crisis every 2.26 years'; a signed 32-bit count of seconds ends at 03:14:07 UTC on 19 January 2038 [P52] [P53]. Leap seconds began in 1972, 27 have been added, the latest on 31 December 2016, and the General Conference on Weights and Measures decided in 2022 that the maximum UT1-UTC difference will be increased in or before 2035; a lawsuit filed on 30 September 2011 shut down the time zone database's mailing list on 6 October 2011 until ICANN took over on 14 October 2011; Thursday 4 October 1582 was followed by Friday 15 October 1582, a gap Python's date type does not have [P55] [P56] [P57] [P58]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P52, chx:P53, chx:P55, chx:P56, chx:P57, chx:P58 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course chapter 19 is best taught as the choice of a clock by the question asked, a monotonic clock for an interval and an aware datetime in a named zone for a moment on a calendar, and the choice of a launcher by the question asked, a list of arguments without a shell when the program is known and the operating system's scheduler when the program must run with no Python process alive [P01] [P10] [P11] [P12] [P13]. The chapter's listings need four repairs before they run, its outputs depend on the zone and the clock of the author, and its short account of scheduling needs the manual pages and the real behaviour of cron that this study recorded [P01] [P02] [P40]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P10, chx:P11, chx:P12, chx:P13, chx:P40 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "40 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "64 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-13T01:45:00; 47 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
